task: dataflow-workers
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/dataflow-workers
worktree: ../okay-wt-dfp
spec: specs/dataflow.md, stage 4b

why: 4a settled what a partial IS — a value with a Schema. This is
  the half that makes the word "distributed" true: a coordinator, a
  worker protocol, and a job running across real operating-system
  processes.

what:
  `Job[P, R]` — the registry entry. A worker is asked for a NAME plus
  Schema-encoded parameters and builds the same plan from them; no
  function ever crosses (Claim 3). The price is stated rather than
  hidden: every worker runs the same artifact.

  Two requests, mirroring exactly what `Flows.fan` already does in
  one process:
    Extent(job, params, part, of)          -> the pre-pass, data-local
    Run(job, params, part, of, bounds)     -> the partial, as bytes
  Two round trips because the SEEDING needs a prefix maximum over
  every partition before any of them may run.

  Transport: a 4-byte length prefix and CBOR. Binary-safe, and a
  partial is already bytes so it is not re-encoded as text.

  `Cluster.run(job, params, workers, parts)` — partition i to
  worker i % n. A worker is `Req => Resp`, which is what
  `Cluster.Worker` has been since P7: in-process, or a socket away.

tests, in the order they must be written:
  1. in-process workers — the protocol's arithmetic, no sockets
  2. socket workers in this JVM — real framing, real bytes
  3. REAL OS PROCESSES — `java -cp <Test/fullClasspath>
     okay.cluster.WorkerMain <port>`, the classpath handed over the
     way okay-cluster already hands the linked JS client's path
  and the bar for all three: the same answer, the same drop count and
  the same merged count as `Flows.fan` in one process.

predict: the protocol will be the easy half and the CLASSPATH the
  awkward one. I also expect the two round trips to look wasteful and
  to be right anyway — a worker that guesses its own bounds cannot
  know what the partitions before it saw, and that is the whole
  content of the seeding theorem.
